DETECTION DECISION TREE
==================================================

Step 1: Check tan(π/4)
├─ If > 1.0 (0x3ff0000000000001)
│  └─ ARCHITECTURE: MIPS
├─ If < 1.0 (0x3fefffffffffffff)
│  └─ Continue to Step 2

Step 2: Check sin(π/6) and cos(π/3)
├─ If BOTH > 0.5
│  ├─ Check atan(1.0) bits
│  ├─ If 0x3fe921fb54442d17
│  │  └─ ARCHITECTURE: ARM or RISC-V
│  ├─ If 0x3fe921fb54442d18
│  │  └─ ARCHITECTURE: Likely not ARM
├─ If BOTH < 0.5
│  ├─ Check atan(1.0) bits
│  ├─ If 0x3fe921fb54442d18
│  │  ├─ ARCHITECTURE: x86 or PPC
│  │  └─ Continue to Step 3
│  ├─ If 0x3fe921fb54442d16
│  │  └─ ARCHITECTURE: MIPS (confirmed)

Step 3: Check Flush-to-Zero (FTZ) mode
├─ Test: 1e-320 / 2.0
├─ If == 0.0 (flushes to zero)
│  ├─ ARCHITECTURE: ARM (confirmed)
│  ├─ PLATFORM: Windows ← Windows ARM detected
│  └─ Bitwidth: Check sizeof(void*)
├─ If > 0.0 (gradual underflow)
│  ├─ ARCHITECTURE: x86/PPC/RISC-V/ARM confirmed
│  └─ Continue to Step 4

Step 4: If ARM with gradual underflow, check exp(1.0) bits
├─ If 0x4005bf0a8b145768
│  ├─ PLATFORM: Linux ← Linux ARM (glibc)
│  ├─ Also check 1/3+1/3+1/3 < 1.0 (confirms glibc)
│  └─ Bitwidth: Check sizeof(void*)
├─ If 0x4005bf0a8b145769
│  ├─ PLATFORM: macOS ← macOS ARM64 (Apple libm)
│  ├─ Also check 1/3+1/3+1/3 ≈ 1.0 (confirms Apple)
│  └─ Bitwidth: 64-bit only (Apple Silicon)

Step 5: If x86 architecture detected
├─ All x86 platforms share same transcendental values
├─ Cannot distinguish Windows/Linux/macOS on x86 via FP alone
├─ Would need secondary detection (endianness, OS calls, etc)
└─ Bitwidth: Check sizeof(void*)


KEY DETECTION SIGNATURES
==================================================

UNIQUE SIGNATURES (by architecture):

MIPS:
- tan(π/4) > 1.0 (ONLY MIPS)
- atan(1.0) = 0x3fe921fb54442d16 (LOWEST)
- exp(1.0) = 0x4005bf0a8b145768
- sin(π/6) < 0.5
- cos(π/3) > 0.5

x86:
- tan(π/4) < 1.0
- atan(1.0) = 0x3fe921fb54442d18 (HIGHEST)
- sin(π/6) < 0.5
- cos(π/3) < 0.5
- 80-bit internal precision effects

ARM:
- tan(π/4) < 1.0
- atan(1.0) = 0x3fe921fb54442d17 (MIDDLE)
- sin(π/6) > 0.5
- cos(π/3) > 0.5
- NO 80-bit extended precision

RISC-V:
- tan(π/4) < 1.0
- atan(1.0) = 0x3fe921fb54442d17 (MIDDLE - same as ARM)
- sin(π/6) > 0.5
- cos(π/3) > 0.5
- sqrt(2) highly precise
- Gradual underflow supported

PowerPC:
- tan(π/4) < 1.0
- atan(1.0) = 0x3fe921fb54442d18 (SAME as x86)
- sin(π/6) < 0.5
- cos(π/3) > 0.5 (UNIQUE: cos differs from sin direction)
- IBM FP spec


PLATFORM SIGNATURES (ARM architecture only):

Windows ARM:
- Flush-to-Zero: ENABLED (1e-320/2 == 0.0)
- This is THE key differentiator
- No gradual underflow allowed

Linux ARM:
- Flush-to-Zero: DISABLED (1e-320/2 > 0.0)
- exp(1.0) = 0x4005bf0a8b145768 (glibc)
- 1/3+1/3+1/3: likely < 1.0

macOS ARM64:
- Flush-to-Zero: DISABLED (1e-320/2 > 0.0)
- exp(1.0) = 0x4005bf0a8b145769 (Apple libm) ← DIFFERENT from Linux
- 1/3+1/3+1/3: 1.00000000000000000 (cleaner rounding)


SUMMARY DETECTION ORDER
==================================================

1. tan(π/4) bits → Distinguish MIPS from all others
2. atan(1.0) bits → Distinguish x86/PPC from ARM/RISC-V
3. sin(π/6) > vs < 0.5 → Distinguish ARM from x86/MIPS/PPC
4. cos(π/3) direction → Distinguish PPC from x86
5. Flush-to-Zero test → Distinguish Windows ARM from Linux/macOS ARM
6. exp(1.0) bits → Distinguish Linux ARM from macOS ARM
7. 1/3+1/3+1/3 precision → Confirm macOS vs Linux
8. sizeof(void*) → Get bitwidth (32 vs 64)
